# Nothing a run leaves belongs in git. The normal state of this directory is
# no run artifact at all -- the numbers live in `runs/runNN.md` and the
# artifacts do not -- so everything here is deleted once a run is written up
# and the deletion accepted. Three pair notes were tracked by accident before
# this file existed, under three names as the scheme changed, and had to be
# taken out of history; that is what this prevents.
#
# The run number is what anchors these patterns: `run[0-9]*` catches the
# binaries, the JSONs, the logs and the pair note, and leaves `run-major.sh`
# and `run-gate.sh` alone, which a bare `run*` would not.
run[0-9]*

# The one thing carrying a run number that IS tracked: the run's write-up,
# which is the document the artifacts are deleted in favour of. `runs/` is
# not itself matched above -- `s` is no digit -- so a re-include reaches
# inside it.
!runs/run[0-9]*.md

# Binaries and notes from before the run went into every name (Runs 10 and 11
# and the probes beside them), kept here so an old artifact cannot be staged
# either.
micro-aligned
micro-unaligned
micro-maxskip*
micro-both
micro-procalign
micro-*-pair.txt
micro-pair*.txt

# Probe artifacts, which are named for the question rather than for a run.
# Broadened from `probe-*.json` and `probe-*.log` on 2026-08-29. A run
# leaves more shapes than those two: a driver a session wrote to launch
# the riders, a pid file, a `-g3` twin binary, a directory of paragraph
# drafts. Run 21 left eight such paths in `git status`, and the two
# suffixes were an accident of which probes came first.
#
# BUT THE PREFIX IS NOT THE WHOLE RULE, which is why the re-includes
# below exist: some probes ARE tracked, being inputs to this README
# rather than artifacts of a run. They are MORE than the re-includes --
# a probe tracked before this pattern was broadened stays tracked, git
# ignoring only what is untracked -- so the list below is the ones a
# delete would strand and not a census of them.
# Ignoring a tracked file is harmless until someone deletes and re-adds
# it, at which point `git add` refuses in silence -- so they are named
# here, and a probe worth tracking in future is named beside them.
probe-*
!probe-areacurve.sh
!probe-within-evening.sh
!probe-order-reversal.sh
!probe-rework-proposal.hs
!probe-oneblock.py
!probe-gate3.py
!probe-flip-reroll.sh
!probe-flip-reroll-read.py
!probe-flip-reroll-fixture.py
!probe-flip-counters.sh
!probe-flip-counters-read.py
!probe-r32-g3-twins.sh

# What those two leave: a directory of legs apiece, named for the question
# and not for a run, so the `run[0-9]*` and `probe-*` patterns miss them.
reroll-out/
reroll-fixture/
counters-out*/
smoke-out/

# The reader's smoke sweep, which writes these and deletes them again; a
# failed sweep can leave them behind. The .log line is not decoration:
# smoke-sweep.sh writes one beside each JSON, and this comment claimed to
# cover them before the pattern did.
smoke*.json
smoke*.log
smoke*.md
README.smoke.*

# Temporary build directories the pair recipes use (`--builddir=db-maxpa`),
# removed by hand after each half is copied out.
db-*/
